<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artaxias IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 422–428</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Artashir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Artaxias IV was the last Arsacid king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, ruling the Sasanian-controlled part of the country. In 428 the Armenian nobles asked the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahram-v">Bahram V</a> to depose him, and the Armenian monarchy was abolished. Eastern Armenia then became the Sasanian province of Persarmenia under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marzban">marzban</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artaxias-iv.jpg" alt="Artaxias IV" width="320" height="285" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ջ. Ֆուզարո · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smbat_bagratunin_tagadrum_e_artashesin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Artaxias IV was the son of Vramshapuh, who ruled eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> as a Sasanid vassal from 389 until 417. His uncle Khosrov IV had ruled Armenia before Vramshapuh, and possibly after him as well, and modern genealogies make Artaxias a grandson of Varasdates. He was born about 405, since he was seventeen years old when he was enthroned. The identity of his mother is unknown. He was born and raised in Armenia, and little is known of his life before he became king.</p><p>When Vramshapuh died in 417, Artaxias was too young to succeed him. The Catholicos Sahak, a distant relative, went to the court of the Sasanid king Yazdegerd I and obtained the release of Khosrov IV from exile, and Khosrov may have reigned again until his death in 418. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> was then ruled directly by the nakharar nobility and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanids</a>, until the nakharars asked for a client king of the Arsacid line and Artaxias was enthroned in 422.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On his accession he took the name Artashir in deference to earlier Sasanid kings, although his own name honoured past kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. He was the third Sasanid vassal of eastern Armenia after his uncle and father, a Christian monarch subject to a Zoroastrian state. The Catholicos Sahak supported him, but the centrifugal tendencies of the nobles were beyond his control.</p><p>The leading nakharars soon resumed their intrigues, claiming disgust at the young king's vices, and his youth and weakness of character left him unable to cope with them. Sahak appealed to the nobles at various times to respect the king's supreme authority and to work with him as his allies, but his appeals were disregarded. The nakharars lost confidence in the monarchy and decided that direct Persian rule was preferable to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>With his removal in 428 Arsacid rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> ended, and with it almost a thousand years of Armenian monarchy: Artaxias was the last person to hold the crown of the ancient Armenian kingdom. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanids</a> installed Veh Mihr Shapur as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marzban">marzban</a> of Persian Armenia. What became of Artaxias after his dethronement is unknown.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Artaxias IV” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 428 Armenian nobles asked the Persian king to remove <b>Artaxias IV</b>, and the monarchy was abolished; the east became Persarmenia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-armenia-arsacid">Kingdom of Armenia (Arsacid)</a></li></ul></section>
